    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d665">

                <pb facs="1223" n="1201" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg1201"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">783.00/1–750: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Legation in Syria
                    </hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d665-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-01-07T17:00:00-05:00">January 7, 1950—5
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8. Brit Emb Wash states FonOff has instructed UK Min<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d665-f2"/> Damascus express to Syrian PriMin<anchor n="4" xml:id="d665-f3"/> Brit concern over recent
                    army coup and apprehension lest instability and army interference in civilian
                    govt may continue.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d665-f4"/> He is to assure Syrian Govt
                    UK policy is one of complete nonintervention and to state UK asking other govts
                    in area fol similar policy in belief Syria must be left alone work out own
                    salvation and restore internal stability. Dept understands Brit Min has taken
                    exception certain aspects his instrs but will
                    probably make approach along these or similar lines.</p>
                <p>Dept likewise concerned over Syrian instability and army interference especially
                    since three successful military coups within nine months have established
                    dangerous precedent which may tempt irresponsible elements in other Near Eastern
                    countries plot violent overthrow ruling govts. Dept wld appreciate ur comments
                    as to whether US expression concern to Syrian Govt over instability in Syria as
                    reflected in three successive military coups wld serve useful purpose of
                    strengthening position civilian authorities. Pls also state whether in ur
                    judgment such a US approach might lend itself to misinterpretation by special
                    groups as indicating unwarranted US interference in Syrian internal affairs and
                    possibly as reflecting US disapproval anti-union orientation recent coup
                    leaders.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d665-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d665-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to London, Cairo, Baghdad, Jidda, Beirut, and Amman.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d665-f2" n="3" xml:id="d665-f2-content">
                <p>P. M. Broadmead.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d665-f3" n="4" xml:id="d665-f3-content">
                <p>Khaled Azm.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d665-f4" n="5" xml:id="d665-f4-content">
                <p>Syria experienced three <hi rendition="#italic">coups d’ etat</hi> in 1949, the
                    last of which occurred on December 19, 1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
